select 'cvoice3xix6wepz2', 'voice-dictation-vs-voice-agents', 'Voice dictation vs voice agents: what is the difference?', 'Both involve talking to a computer, and people mix them up. They solve different problems, and picking the wrong one is the most common mistake in voice projects.', $j$[{"type":"p","text":"\"Voice\" now covers two very different kinds of software. One listens and writes down what you say. The other listens, thinks and answers. If you are planning a voice project, knowing which one you need saves weeks."},{"type":"h2","text":"Dictation: speech in, text out"},{"type":"p","text":"Dictation tools turn your speech into text in whatever field your cursor is in. You talk, the words appear, and you decide what to do with them. The tool does not answer you and does not act on your behalf."},{"type":"p","text":"Good dictation does more than transcribe. It removes filler words, fixes punctuation and handles corrections as you speak, so the text reads like something you wrote. Wispr Flow is an example of this kind of tool."},{"type":"p","text":"Dictation is the right choice when:"},{"type":"ul","items":["A person is going to read, edit or send the text.","The task is writing: messages, documents, prompts, notes, code comments.","You want to speed up something people already do by keyboard."]},{"type":"h2","text":"Voice agents: a conversation with software"},{"type":"p","text":"A voice agent holds a spoken conversation. It listens to the user, works out what they want, decides what to do and replies out loud. A typical agent connects three parts:"},{"type":"ul","items":["**Speech to text** converts what the caller said into words.","**A language model** reasons about the request, often with tools such as a calendar, a database or an API.","**Text to speech** turns the reply back into a voice."]},{"type":"p","text":"A good agent also handles turn-taking, which means knowing when the person has finished speaking and letting them interrupt. Getting that right is much harder than it sounds, and it is where most voice products win or lose."},{"type":"p","text":"Voice agents are the right choice when:"},{"type":"ul","items":["The user should not need to read or type anything.","The task is a back and forth, such as booking, support or guided help.","The person is busy, on the move or not comfortable with text."]},{"type":"h2","text":"Where they differ in practice"},{"type":"ul","items":["**Latency.** For dictation, a short delay before text appears is fine. For an agent, a long pause makes the conversation feel broken.","**Errors.** In dictation, a mistake appears on screen and the user fixes it. In an agent, a misheard word can send the conversation down the wrong path with no visible trace.","**Trust.** An agent that takes actions, such as booking or paying, needs confirmation steps. A dictation tool never acts.","**Cost and complexity.** Dictation is a tool you use. A voice agent is a system you build and operate."]},{"type":"h2","text":"Which should you build with?"},{"type":"p","text":"Many good projects use both. A developer can dictate their way through building an app, and the app itself can be a voice agent for a farmer, a patient or a shopkeeper. The two sit at different ends of the same project: one speeds up the person who builds, the other serves the person who uses."},{"type":"p","text":"If you are not sure, ask a single question: should the software talk back? If yes, you are building an agent. If no, you probably need dictation, or just a better text box."}]$j$::jsonb, 'PUBLISHED', 3, ARRAY['voice','wispr-flow','bolo-and-build']::text[], false, false, now() - interval '40 minutes', (select id from "User" where email='ud.ai.journey@gmail.com'), (select id from "Topic" where slug='ai'), now(), now()
